WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.710
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:09.350
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:16.981
Unit twenty-five: Digital Rights and Privacy. Today you will shorten relative clauses with -ing and -ed participles.

4
00:00:17.281 --> 00:00:19.934
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.261
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:26.385
Digital rights.

7
00:00:27.383 --> 00:00:31.635
Digital rights means the rights people have when they use the internet and digital devices.

8
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.620
Digital rights include privacy and free expression online.

9
00:00:36.217 --> 00:00:38.915
Your turn. Say: Digital rights.

10
00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:43.070
Digital rights.

11
00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:44.736
Privacy.

12
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:49.189
Privacy means the right to keep personal information to yourself.

13
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:51.945
Privacy is a basic human right.

14
00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.144
Your turn. Say: Privacy.

15
00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.059
Privacy.

16
00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:01.062
Data protection.

17
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:06.058
Data protection means laws and methods that keep personal information safe.

18
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:10.170
Data protection law requires companies to store data securely.

19
00:01:10.783 --> 00:01:14.051
Your turn. Say: Data protection.

20
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.052
Data protection.

21
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:19.969
Surveillance.

22
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:24.515
Surveillance means close watching of people, often in secret.

23
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:27.511
Mass surveillance raised public concern.

24
00:01:28.117 --> 00:01:30.676
Your turn. Say: Surveillance.

25
00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:34.674
Surveillance.

26
00:01:35.417 --> 00:01:36.406
Consent.

27
00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:40.554
Consent means permission that a person freely gives.

28
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:44.032
Apps must ask for consent before collecting data.

29
00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:47.072
Your turn. Say: Consent.

30
00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:50.865
Consent.

31
00:01:51.617 --> 00:01:52.954
Online speech.

32
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.470
Online speech means what people say and publish on the internet.

33
00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:01.209
Online speech should be free but responsible.

34
00:02:01.817 --> 00:02:04.504
Your turn. Say: Online speech.

35
00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:08.844
Online speech.

36
00:02:09.583 --> 00:02:10.724
Regulation.

37
00:02:11.717 --> 00:02:14.947
Regulation means an official rule made by a government body.

38
00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.513
A new regulation limits how data may be sold.

39
00:02:19.117 --> 00:02:21.873
Your turn. Say: Regulation.

40
00:02:24.733 --> 00:02:25.978
Regulation.

41
00:02:26.717 --> 00:02:27.904
Platforms.

42
00:02:28.917 --> 00:02:33.238
Platforms means websites or apps where people share content and services.

43
00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.268
Platforms gather huge amounts of user data.

44
00:02:36.883 --> 00:02:39.466
Your turn. Say: Platforms.

45
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.208
Platforms.

46
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.639
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:48.483 --> 00:02:54.561
Visal, a report says 'Data collected by platforms is often sold.' Where is 'which is'?

48
00:02:55.650 --> 00:03:10.089
It is omitted. A reduced relative clause uses a participle: data (which is) collected by platforms. We use -ed for passive meaning and -ing for active meaning: users sharing personal details.

49
00:03:11.183 --> 00:03:12.939
When can we reduce a clause?

50
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:24.945
When the relative pronoun is the subject. We say 'laws protecting privacy' for 'laws which protect privacy', and 'consent given online' for 'consent which is given online'.

51
00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.212
Why is it popular in legal writing?

52
00:03:29.317 --> 00:03:38.680
It is concise. 'Regulation governing online speech' and 'surveillance conducted without a warrant' save words and keep sentences clear.

53
00:03:39.833 --> 00:03:41.171
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:42.017 --> 00:03:46.466
Grammar focus: Reduced relative clauses: -ing and -ed.

55
00:03:46.766 --> 00:03:47.976
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:48.683 --> 00:03:52.378
Data collected by platforms is often sold to advertisers.

57
00:03:53.283 --> 00:03:55.956
Laws protecting privacy are being updated.

58
00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:01.044
Surveillance conducted without consent violates digital rights.

59
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:12.036
Reduce a relative clause with -ing (active) or -ed (passive): data (which is) collected..., laws (which) protect → laws protecting. Only when the pronoun is the subject.

60
00:04:12.783 --> 00:04:17.166
Be careful! Don't say: Users shared data should be careful.

61
00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.610
Say: Users sharing data should be careful.

62
00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:23.736
Use -ing for an active meaning.

63
00:04:24.533 --> 00:04:26.872
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:28.981
Your Data, Your Rights.

65
00:04:29.281 --> 00:04:33.162
Digital rights are the basic freedoms people expect when using the internet.

66
00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:38.871
Personal data collected by platforms can reveal habits, opinions and locations.

67
00:04:39.867 --> 00:04:45.198
Regulation requiring clear consent, and laws protecting privacy, give users some control.

68
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:51.775
Surveillance conducted in secret, even for security reasons, risks harming online speech.

69
00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:57.611
People wanting safe services should read what they agree to, and governments should keep rules simple.

70
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.771
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:03.617 --> 00:05:06.115
Data blank by platforms is often sold.

72
00:05:06.415 --> 00:05:10.313
A, collecting. B, collected. C, collect.

73
00:05:13.867 --> 00:05:16.180
The answer is B: collected.

74
00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:19.711
Laws blank privacy are being updated.

75
00:05:20.011 --> 00:05:23.746
A, protecting. B, protected. C, protect.

76
00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.532
The answer is A: protecting.

77
00:05:30.683 --> 00:05:33.147
Permission that a user freely gives is blank.

78
00:05:33.447 --> 00:05:37.461
A, surveillance. B, regulation. C, consent.

79
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.756
The answer is C: consent.

80
00:05:43.917 --> 00:05:45.638
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:45.938 --> 00:05:52.946
A, Users shared data should be careful.. B, Users sharing data should be careful.. C, Users share data should be careful..

82
00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:59.939
The answer is B: Users sharing data should be careful..

83
00:06:01.083 --> 00:06:04.940
According to the reading, secret surveillance risks harming online…

84
00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.708
A, games. B, prices. C, speech.

85
00:06:12.267 --> 00:06:14.289
The answer is C: speech.

86
00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.338
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:19.183 --> 00:06:22.239
How much personal data should apps be allowed to collect?

88
00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.515
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:32.815 --> 00:07:00.133
Apps should collect only the data they need to provide their service, and they should explain clearly what they take and why. Users should give real consent, not hidden in long terms, and be able to delete their data. Sensitive information, such as location or health records, needs extra protection. Strong data protection rules keep convenience from becoming surveillance.

90
00:07:00.433 --> 00:07:02.281
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:03.183 --> 00:07:08.599
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and reduced relative clauses: -ing and -ed.

92
00:07:08.899 --> 00:07:14.163
Today's value is digital responsibility. Use technology in ways that protect others.

93
00:07:14.463 --> 00:07:21.797
Congratulations! You have finished Online Newspaper Class, level three. See you in Online Newspaper Class, level four!

94
00:07:22.097 --> 00:07:24.657
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

